1.16.0: the film is made of grains - silver grain and edge effects

--grain silver on polajuice and superjuice, GRAIN= in super8ify, a
selector on the page: grain generated from exposure inside the film
rather than laid over the finished picture. Binomial developed-fraction
statistics set the amplitude (paper white and deep black stay quiet),
and texture follows exposure - the large fast crystals develop first,
so shadows are carried by the coarse population and highlights fill in
fine. It forms before the print stock, push and age, which act on it as
they act on the image; color stock gets three partly correlated dye
layers with the fast blue-sensitive top layer grainiest. Mid gray
carries the same RMS as classic for the same camera and film, so the
1.14 film-stem grain ladder carries over. Classic remains the default.

--edge 0..2 (EDGE= in super8ify, a slider on the page): development
edge effects. Developer exhausts locally, so the dense side of a
boundary gains density (border), the thin side loses a little (fringe)
and a small dense detail gains most (Eberhard). First-order steady state
D' = D(1 - g Dbar)/(1 - g D) over a 0.15 mm diffusion length on the
35mm diagonal, scaled with the image: multiplicative and asymmetric,
not an unsharp mask, and uniform areas map exactly to themselves. 1 is
a dilute developer, 2 stand development. Silver stock applies one gain
on luma, color stock works per dye layer (DIR interimage behavior).
Ignored for instant cameras, with the usual note.

Core tests: silver grain correlates neighbors more in a dark field than
a bright one while classic shows no trend, renders are deterministic,
unknown models refused; edge leaves a uniform field alone to 2e-3 and
raises a small dense detail by >2% while the distant field is
unchanged. Movie, wasm, page and DOM suites cover their layers. Model
constants disclosed in PRESET_SOURCES; ROADMAP's exposure-dependent
grain item closed.

Also: test_compat.sh no longer uses GNU mktemp --suffix (BSD mktemp
lacks it, so every cell refused on macOS); release.sh untracks the .d
dependency files and the superjuice binary that had slipped into
history and dirtied the tree on every make.

Film-family inference now looks only below the library root: macOS
temp roots live under /var/folders/XX/ and a user whose XX was "bw"
had Portra refused from 35mm-negative as black-and-white film.
test_compat.sh runs its stub library under a /bw/ root as the
regression.
